%I A401146 #15 Oct 23 2026 10:13:34
%S A401146 1,2,3,10,32,4,5,16,6,20,64,7,22,68,208,640,2048,8,9,28,88,272,832,
%T A401146 2560,8192,10,32,11,34,104,320,1024,12,40,128,13,40,128,14,44,136,416,
%U A401146 1280,4096,15,46,140,424,1280,4096,16,17,52,160,512,18,56,176,544,1664,5120,16384
%N A401146 Irregular triangle read by rows: row n lists the iterates of A302338 starting at n, up to the first power of 2.
%C A401146 Row n is n, A302338(n), A302338(A302338(n)), ..., stopping at the first term that is a power of 2. Row n is finite if and only if the Collatz trajectory of n reaches 1; in that case row n has A006667(n) + 1 = A078719(n) terms and its last term is 2^A006666(n) (see the comment in A302338).
%C A401146 Writing T(n,k) = 2^e*m with m odd, m is the (k+1)-th odd term of the Collatz trajectory of n (A006370) and e is the number of halving steps that precede it in that trajectory.
%C A401146 T(n,k) = 3^k*n + Sum_{j=0..k-1} 3^(k-1-j)*2^e(j), where e(j) = A007814(T(n,j)): each "+1" step of the trajectory stays as a separate term. For the last term of row n this reads 2^A006666(n) = 3^A006667(n)*n + A400965(n).
%C A401146 Dividing by T(n,k) writes 1, at every level k, as 3^k*n/T(n,k) plus the k terms 3^(k-1-j)*2^e(j)/T(n,k); the ratio of the first part to the term j = 0 is 3*n/2^e(0) (3*n for odd n), and the ratio of the term j to the term j+1 is 3/2^(e(j+1)-e(j)).
%H A401146 Carlos Alberto Terencio de Bastos, <a href="/A401146/b401146.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..9985</a> (rows 1..538, flattened)
%H A401146 Carlos Alberto Terencio de Bastos, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.19861962">The Bastos tree and the Collatz Conjecture: the composed 1 reveals the path</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A401146 <a href="/index/3#3x1">Index entries for sequences related to 3x+1 (or Collatz) problem</a>
%F A401146 T(n,0) = n; T(n,k+1) = A302338(T(n,k)) = 3*T(n,k) + 2^A007814(T(n,k)).
%F A401146 T(2*n,k) = 2*T(n,k).
%e A401146 Triangle begins:
%e A401146   n=1: 1;
%e A401146   n=2: 2;
%e A401146   n=3: 3, 10, 32;
%e A401146   n=4: 4;
%e A401146   n=5: 5, 16;
%e A401146   n=6: 6, 20, 64;
%e A401146   n=7: 7, 22, 68, 208, 640, 2048;
%e A401146   n=8: 8;
%e A401146   n=9: 9, 28, 88, 272, 832, 2560, 8192;
%e A401146   n=10: 10, 32;
%e A401146   n=11: 11, 34, 104, 320, 1024;
%e A401146 For n = 11: 34 = 3*11 + 1, 104 = 3*34 + 2, 320 = 3*104 + 8, 1024 = 3*320 + 64 = 2^10. Writing each term as 2^e*m with m odd gives (e, m) = (0, 11), (1, 17), (3, 13), (6, 5), (10, 1): 11, 17, 13, 5, 1 are the odd terms of the Collatz trajectory of 11, reached after 0, 1, 3, 6 and 10 halving steps. Also 1024 = 3^4*11 + 133, where 133 = 27 + 18 + 24 + 64 = A400965(11).
%t A401146 A401146row[n_] := NestWhileList[3*# + 2^IntegerExponent[#, 2] &, n, BitAnd[#, # - 1] > 0 &];
%t A401146 Array[A401146row, 20] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Oct 23 2026 *)
%o A401146 (Python)
%o A401146 def row(n):
%o A401146     r = [n]
%o A401146     while r[-1] & (r[-1] - 1):
%o A401146         q = r[-1]
%o A401146         r.append(3*q + (q & -q))
%o A401146     return r
%o A401146 print([t for n in range(1, 19) for t in row(n)])
%Y A401146 Cf. A000265, A006370, A006666, A006667, A007814, A078719 (row lengths), A302338, A351122, A400965.
%K A401146 nonn,tabf,look,new
%O A401146 1,2
%A A401146 _Carlos Alberto Terencio de Bastos_, Oct 16 2026
